// Exponential map: a rotation vector -- axis * angle, packed into one `Vec3` --
// to the unit quaternion of that rotation. `exp` in the Lie-group sense, and
// the inverse of `se.kin.QuatLog`.
//
//     exp(v) = ( cos(|v|/2), sin(|v|/2) * v/|v| )
//
// Named for the map, but the field this library serves calls the operation the
// ROTATION VECTOR to quaternion conversion -- the C++ this is ported from names
// it `fromRotationVector`, MATLAB names it `rotvec2quat`, and the strapdown
// navigation literature calls `v` the rotation vector throughout. It is NOT the
// axis-angle conversion: that name conventionally means the four-number (unit
// axis, scalar angle) pair, and a block taking that pair is a different block
// this one does not foreclose.
//
// This is the block an attitude integrator is built out of. Integrating a body
// rate means composing `exp(omega*dt)` onto the current orientation each step,
// and for a rate held constant across the step that composition is not an
// approximation of the kinematics -- it is their closed-form solution. The
// alternative, integrating the four quaternion components against
// `qdot = 1/2 q (x) omega` and renormalising afterwards, is a first-order
// method wearing a correction that hides how far off it drifted.
//
// The output is unit-norm by construction: cos^2 + sin^2 * |v/|v||^2 = 1, in
// exact arithmetic and to the last bit in floating point. Nothing downstream
// has to renormalise, and no contract here needs to assert it (see below).
//
// The unit is FIXED at `rad` rather than parameterised, unlike `math.Norm3` or
// `math.Cross3`. Those generalise because a magnitude or a product genuinely
// has a dimension for the caller to choose. A rotation vector does not: it is
// an angle and can be nothing else. Fixing it is what turns the most likely
// wiring mistake at this port -- a `Vec3(rad/s)` reaching it because a `*dt`
// was forgotten -- into an SE0410 rather than a plausible-looking orientation
// that is wrong by a factor of the step size.
//
// COORDINATE CONVENTION -- right-handed, matching `math.Vec3` and the rotation
// matrix `se.kin.QuatToRot` builds. As `Vec3` says, this is documentation: the
// compiler does not model frames and cannot check that the vector arriving here
// is expressed in the one the caller thinks it is.
package se.kin;

use se.math;
use se.kin;

node QuatExp {

    inputs  { v: math.Vec3(rad); }
    outputs { q: kin.Quat; }

    output(v) {
        const double th2 = in.v.x*in.v.x + in.v.y*in.v.y + in.v.z*in.v.z;
        const double th  = std::sqrt(th2);

        // k = sin(th/2)/th, by its Taylor series below the crossover. The
        // series is not an optimisation. At th = 0 the closed form is 0/0, and
        // an identity rotation is the single most common input this block ever
        // sees -- every step before the first motion, and every axis of a rate
        // that happens to be zero. The next term is th^4/3840, so 1e-8 leaves
        // the branch's two sides agreeing far below what a double can resolve.
        const double k = th > 1e-8 ? std::sin(0.5*th)/th : 0.5 - th2/48.0;

        out.q.w = std::cos(0.5*th);
        out.q.x = k * in.v.x;
        out.q.y = k * in.v.y;
        out.q.z = k * in.v.z;

        // `math.Norm3`'s argument, one layer earlier. Unit norm needs no
        // contract -- it is exact by construction, so asserting it would only
        // ever fire on a compiler bug. What is worth asserting is the input,
        // because the caller downstream is an orientation STATE: a single
        // non-finite rate poisons it for the rest of the run, and by the time
        // it surfaces as a column of NaN there is no node's name left on it
        // (SPEC 10.6). One check on the magnitude, not three on the components:
        // a non-finite component reaches `th` through the sum whatever it was.
        sim.check_finite(th, "the rotation angle is finite");
    }
}
